 long way and you  know there were uh in fact a new first  year graduate student named lily in the  lab has achieved 100 editing at multiple  target sites uh in including in in  patient-derived uh inpatient fibroblasts  primary fibroblasts so  uh so based on has come a long way and  and there were moments  early on even  right when we  published the paper in 2016  where we wondered are we ever going to  be able to achieve  you know  80 90 100 editing even though i think  for the vast majority of genetic  diseases to benefit patients you don't  need editing that efficient  um but the answer actually is yes you  can get close to 100 base editing  using the variety of improvements that  the community  uh have now advanced from our original  work in 2016.  and i should also mention that  two groups in japan  professors nishida and kondo working  together  reported in science several months after  our initial base editor paper that  alexis led  reported in science a similar system  where they used a different deaminase  uh but also with cas9 also nicking the  opposite strand  and reported similar results so  that was also welcome news because it  really showed that  um that you know independent groups were  were finding the same  uh outcomes that you could now make for  the first time targeted  single base pair changes without cutting  the dna double helix without cutting a  chromosome into pieces  in a living mammalian cell in a in a  living human cell  and that really of course got us  fixated on okay let's figure out how to  how to fix as many possible types of  mutations that are responsible for  genetic disease and that researchers  might want to install in cells to study  the properties of genes  as possible but the boundary conditions  are you can't make double stranded  breaks  well no that's really incredible to hear  the the backstory to how base editing  started and it's incredible also to hear  that you're approaching 100 efficiency  um  which is definitely um pretty amazing  considering  um like where it started with um and say  obviously you've now taken um base  editing uh to beam therapeutics which uk  founded and you're trying to  now take um the the basic science and  discovery and taking it to being able to  treat different genetic diseases  and so um  out of the different genetic diseases  what um what do you think based editing  will be used for first in terms of  getting to  patients in the clinic  yeah well so beam has disclosed a  variety of of programs that they  plan to bring to the clinic  this year or shortly there afterwards  and  based on their announcements  they've disclosed that they are  they have developed base editing  strategies to treat  some blood diseases  they've developed base editing to base  editing strategies to  treat some metabolic diseases as well  like  alfie alpha-1 anti-trypsin  deficiency  and i think they've also disclosed that  they have  been able to base at it to correct some  genetic alleles that are involved in  some blindness diseases as well  i  always want to make sure that  anything i'm sharing is something that  they've already shared so  just point people to the beam  therapeutics website where they i think  list the programs that they've publicly  disclosed  but i think maybe from from a more meta  perspective  uh  you know a decision that i admire that  that beam has made is uh  they recognize that the platform  was so broad  in its potential applicability to  diseases that they would not focus on  just one  organ one type of disease one delivery  modality  but instead  beam has i think done a great job trying  to  advance as many programs as possible to  cover the broadest possible range of  of diseases and therefore of patients  that could benefit from base editing  technology  and so they have  really chosen to  to pursue as many programs that meet um  you know some key criteria like is there  bulletproof biology that relates the  correction of the mutation to the  correction of the disease  uh is there a delivery modality that can  uh that is promising to deliver the base  editors to  the cells that are anticipated to  lead to  disease rescue if corrected  and  you know what are the  uh  uh what are the potential base editing  strategies  that can fix the mutation with the best  current base editing technologies known  and you know fortunately i think is a  sign of of the progress that has led to  literally hundreds of base editors now  being published  uh having been published  that third criteria  is increasingly proving easier and  easier to me  that with all of the different kas9 pam  variants  uh the different cast proteins that have  proven to be compatible with pace  editors including a variety of cast 12  variants  that uh targeting is  is increasingly  uh  uh not an issue  and uh  and even sort of positioning the target  nucleotide with respect to the base  editing window has also been simplified  not just by the fact that there are now  lots of different pamp sequences that  you can target  that is lots of places in the genome  where you can park a base editor  but researchers have started to have  some  success uh controlling where the base  editing window is and how wide or how  narrow it is  and so that diversity of literally  hundreds of base editors that are  available now  um  greatly increase the chance that if you  have a mutation you're trying to correct  uh you can do so with uh with a base  editor without too much trouble well  then that's super exciting because yeah  that's what i'm um obviously the  concerns for base editing is you  obviously have cas9 coming into the dna  site  opening up the dna region and obviously  in theory there could be  potential for of target editing of  different sites so having control over  that window is a great advance  right and and uh researchers have shown  that you can  both  mutate the deaminases to  uh control that window and you can also  mutate the cas9  and in in maybe intellectually at least  my favorite  solution you can circularly permute cas9  proteins something that dave savage has  had success with and so we collaborated  with  with his lab and showed that these  circularly permuted  cast nines do indeed change enough the  topology of the r loop relative to the  deaminase that you can  move  in some cases broaden in other cases  actually move over  the base editing window  so  so having a sort of bookshelf with lots  and lots of different books that each  have slightly different properties  has proven to be  especially useful for  enabling base editing to  serve as many purposes as possible it is  at as many target sites as  as pos as possible with as many  performance characteristics as is needed  to  enable the research or the therapeutic  application of interest  you
 

